      injected scripts are /_vercel/insights/script.js and
      /_vercel/speed-insights/script.js on our own host. That is precisely why
      these are acceptable and an ad stack is not — a visitor watching the
      Network tab to check their photo is not uploaded sees first-party
      requests, not a wall of third-party calls.
  [x] Neither can observe anything derived from the image; there is no API
      surface through which it could reach them.
  [x] Injected client-side on mount, so they add nothing to the server-rendered
      HTML and cannot delay first paint.
  [ ] The privacy page (#34) must state plainly that page-view and performance
      analytics exist, and that neither ever sees the photo. Claiming "nothing
      is collected" would be false.

PR #13 — PhotoSpec schema, Zod validation, registry (US, UK, Schengen)
Branch: feat/photospec-registry        Depends on: #12                Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  The country requirement model. This registry is the moat; the schema decides
  how painful the next 37 countries will be.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] PhotoSpec Zod schema with required provenance, plus a `verification`
      field — see below, it is the most important thing in this task
  [x] Registry keyed by country:document, parsed and frozen at module load
  [x] Schengen base spec authored as a RATIO, because that is the unit the
      standard publishes, plus a deep-merge for per-state overrides
  [x] Validation at registry construction: a malformed or duplicate spec throws
      before anything can serve it. `npm run validate:specs` in CI.
  [x] Staleness helper with an injected clock, so no test can go flaky by
      crossing the boundary on a particular day
  [x] findSpecIn takes its registry, keeping the lookup a pure function

  NEW CONCEPT — `verification: 'verified' | 'provisional'`
  A spec nobody has checked against the issuing authority's own page is
  PROVISIONAL and is not served. All four seeded specs are provisional: the
  values were authored from known figures, but the build environment cannot
  reach travel.state.gov or gov.uk, so nothing has been confirmed at source.
  Presenting an unverified government requirement as authoritative is the exact
  failure this product exists to prevent, so it is a state in the type system
  rather than a caveat in a comment.
  CONSEQUENCE: no country page will render until a human verifies a spec and
  flips the flag. That is intentional.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Inverted bands rejected: head height, eye line and digital edge sizes
  [x] Head height normalised once at the registry boundary and returned in BOTH
      units, with the authored unit preserved so a citation can quote what the
      authority actually published rather than our conversion of it
  [x] Deep merge for Schengen overrides. A shallow spread would replace the
      whole print or background object when a state overrides one field of it,
      silently dropping every sibling key — the spec would still parse, it
      would just be wrong.
  [x] Typed not-found that distinguishes an unknown country from an unsupported
      document, so the page can say the right thing
  [x] Deep-frozen at registry construction
  [x] Two documents in one country (us:passport and us:visa both seeded)
  [x] alternativePrintSizes for authorities accepting more than one
  [x] Source URL required by the schema; a spec without one fails to parse
  [x] Duplicate country:document pairs throw rather than silently overwriting

SEO
  [ ] lastVerified feeds sitemap lastmod and the visible "verified on" line

TESTS
  Unit: schema validation, merge logic, staleness, every seeded spec parses.
  Fixture: a deliberately invalid spec proves the build gate works.

