PR #15a — CI gate repair, and the accessibility defect it uncovered
Branch: feat/lighthouse-budgets         Depends on: #15               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Unplanned, inserted rather than numbered, so the plan below keeps its
  numbering. PR #15 got the build running on CI for the first time since #2;
  the jobs behind it then failed for their own reasons, each one a gate that
  had never actually executed.

DESCRIPTION
  Three CI jobs were decorative. This makes them real, and fixes what they
  found the moment they ran.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] @lhci/cli installed. The budgets job ran `lhci autorun` with the package
      never added, so it exited 127 every time.
  [x] lighthouserc.cjs with thresholds calibrated against a measured run —
      performance 1.00, LCP 0.5s, CLS 0, TBT 0ms — rather than guessed. A
      budget that fails on the day it lands teaches everyone to mute the job.
  [x] test:e2e builds Storybook first. The Playwright config serves
      storybook-static for every project, not just the stories one, so the app
      specs could never start on a clean checkout. CI's separate story step is
      now redundant and removed.
  [x] app/icon.svg. Chrome's automatic /favicon.ico request was 404ing on every
      page load and counting against best-practices.
  [x] eslint-plugin-react declared directly, and react/no-multi-comp scoped to
      *.tsx. Adding one .cjs file at the root took the whole lint suite down:
      the rule reached a file eslint-config-next's globs do not cover, where
      the borrowed plugin was unregistered.

  ACCESSIBILITY DEFECT (found by the gate, on its first real run)
  [x] --tk-text-tertiary was 4.34:1 on white — below WCAG AA. Lighthouse saw
      only that one, because it measures what the single page it visits
      happens to render. Measuring every pair found three more: status-warn at
      3.94:1 on sunken, status-pass at 4.40:1, status-manual at 4.49:1.
  [x] All four re-solved in HSL, hue and saturation preserved, darkened until
      each clears 4.5:1 against the darkest surface it can land on.
  [x] token-contrast.test.ts asserts every foreground token against every
      surface, in all three theme states — 72 pairs, independent of whether a
      component using them exists yet. Verified by reverting a token and
      watching it fail.
  [x] A separate assertion that the primary > secondary > tertiary hierarchy
      survives, so a future contrast fix cannot silently flatten it.

LATENT — the visual suite depends on the machine's fonts
  [ ] --tk-font-mono is `ui-monospace, 'SF Mono', Menlo, monospace`, which
      resolves to whatever the machine has. Six of the sixteen baselines this
      PR regenerated changed only because the container was rebuilt, not
      because any design changed.
      CI then ran the visual job for the first time and passed against those
      same baselines, so the GitHub runner and the dev container currently
      agree. That makes this latent rather than active: nothing is red, and
      nothing guarantees the next runner image resolves the same font. Worth
      self-hosting before the suite grows, not urgent while it is green.

TESTS
  Unit: contrast maths against published values, and the token invariant.
  All gates green locally: typecheck, lint, 698+ unit tests at 100% coverage,
  build, validate:specs, test:seo, 129 Playwright specs, lhci.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #16 — Image ingestion: decode, EXIF orientation, downscale
Branch: feat/image-ingestion           Depends on: #15               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Turning whatever the user gives us into a normalised bitmap. More failure
  modes live here than anywhere else in the product.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Validation ordered by cost: size before content, content before decode,
      dimensions only once something has been decoded
  [x] EXIF orientation and capture time, read by a purpose-built reader rather
      than a dependency parsing 300 tags — two fields is short enough to
      bounds-check completely
  [x] Working-size planning that never upscales, with an exact scale factor
      back to the source so the crop is always computed at full resolution
  [x] Typed failures that each carry a remedy, not just a message
  [x] ImageDecoder seam with a deterministic fake, mirroring the Detector
      pattern from #15 — every decision around the decode is tested; only the
      decode itself needs a browser

EDGE CASES
  [x] HEIC/HEIF — attempted rather than refused. Safari on iOS decodes it
      natively and iOS is where these files come from, so refusing on format
      alone would turn away the exact user whose phone produced the file. When
      the browser genuinely cannot, the message is the four taps that fix it,
      not the word "unsupported".
  [x] All eight EXIF orientation values, including the four mirrored ones, in
      both byte orders — 16 round-trip assertions. A big-endian SHORT read as
      little-endian turns orientation 6 into 0x0600.
  [x] Images with no EXIF at all, and files whose Exif segment sits behind a
      JFIF segment, as most cameras write them
  [x] Animated GIF/WebP refused rather than silently using frame one — a still
      photo is what the user needs to submit
  [x] Dimensions beyond the browser canvas limit, checked against the
      orientation-corrected size rather than the stored one
  [x] Corrupt, truncated and zero-byte files: every offset bounds-checked, and
      a sweep asserting every truncation length of a real file parses without
      throwing
  [x] Extension lying about content — no filename or MIME type is passed to
      the validator at all. A renamed HEIC is the most common upload on iOS.
  [x] Dead camera clocks (0000:00:00), impossible dates, and date entries
      declaring the wrong type
  [x] EXIF capture date for the recency hint, read from DateTimeOriginal
      rather than DateTime, which any edit rewrites

  DEFERRED, with reasons
  [ ] CMYK JPEGs, 16-bit PNGs, alpha, progressive JPEGs and ICC colour
      profiles — all decided inside createImageBitmap and a canvas draw. They
      belong with the browser decoder implementation (#25, with camera
      capture), where there is something real to test them against; asserting
      them behind the fake would be asserting the fake.
  [ ] Memory behaviour on 48MP photos — measurable only in a real browser.
      The guard that matters is already here: an oversized file is refused
      before a decoder allocates anything.
  [ ] Integration: real uploads through the UI in Playwright — needs the
      upload UI (#26).

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  112 unit tests across six files, 100% coverage with no suppressions.
  JPEG files with EXIF are synthesised by a builder rather than committed as
  binary, for the same reason the head fixtures are generated: the input is
  described in words, so a failure names the property that broke instead of
  sending someone to a hex editor. Both byte orders and all eight orientations
  come free, and the mirrored ones — which real corpora almost never contain —
  are as easy to produce as upright ones.

NOTE
  Two lint changes came with this. The format signatures moved out of
  magic-bytes.utils.ts into image-format.constants.ts, and no-magic-numbers is
  now scoped off *.constants.ts and the fixtures directory rather than
  suppressed inline — a file of transcribed specification values is where
  named numbers belong, and the rule still covers every *.utils.ts and
  component. The one pre-existing eslint-disable comment in the codebase is
  gone as a result.


═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 PHASE 3 — THE ANALYSIS ENGINE
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #17 — Face landmark detection
Branch: feat/face-landmarks            Depends on: #16               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  MediaPipe Face Landmarker behind the detector interface from PR #15.

MEASURED COST OF A FIRST CHECK — the number that shapes the UI
  WASM runtime   11.76 MB raw -> 3.42 MB gzipped
  Landmark model  3.76 MB raw -> 3.33 MB gzipped (float16; barely compresses)
  --------------------------------------------------------------
  Total          15.52 MB raw -> 6.75 MB gzipped, less again with brotli

  All of it lazy, so LCP is untouched and the client bundle stayed at 948 KB
  with no MediaPipe in any chunk — verified, not assumed. But it is also the
  whole of the wait before a first result, which is why the loader reports real
  progress rather than a spinner, why the Cache API copy matters so much, and
  why warming on pointerenter is worth building.

SELF-HOSTED, NOT GOOGLE'S CDN
  A privacy decision before a performance one. The product's claim is that the
  photo never leaves the device; a third-party request from the checker page
  would mean Google sees an IP and a timestamp correlatable with "this person
  is checking a passport photo". Serving the same bytes from our own origin
  leaves nothing to explain, and removes a DNS lookup, a TLS handshake and
  somebody else's uptime from the critical path.

  scripts/fetch-models.mjs populates public/models at build time: the runtime
  is copied from node_modules so it can never drift from the installed API,
  and the model is downloaded and verified against a pinned SHA-256. Pinned by
  content, not just URL — the URL is mutable and belongs to somebody else, and
  a model that silently changed would move every measurement this product
  makes without a single test failing. Output is gitignored.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Lazy model load, Cache API persistence, load-progress events
  [x] GPU delegate with CPU fallback
  [x] Landmarks, blendshapes and pose recovered from the transformation matrix
  [x] Typed result with a confidence score
  [x] Face selection separated from the model adapter, so the "which face"
      decision is testable on its own

EDGE CASES
  [x] No face detected
  [x] Multiple faces — largest wins, and the caller is told there were others.
      numFaces is 2 rather than 1 on purpose: asking for one lets the model
      pick by confidence, so a sharply-focused bystander can beat the subject.
  [x] Face too small to measure — below 15% of frame height a one-pixel
      landmark error is several millimetres of head height, larger than any
      specification tolerance
  [x] Face touching the frame edge, where landmarks are extrapolated
  [x] Extreme yaw/roll, with the threshold deliberately generous: it decides
      whether measuring is worth doing, not whether the rule passes
  [x] Model download fails, is slow, or the user is offline
  [x] A truncated model refused rather than loaded — a short model produces
      silently wrong landmarks, far worse than failing
  [x] WebGL unavailable => CPU path, asserted
  [x] Cache API absent, throwing on open, throwing on read, and failing on
      quota — four separate tests, because private browsing does all four
  [x] Two loads racing on a double-click share one request
  [x] A failed load does not poison the retry

  DEFERRED, with reasons
  [ ] Sunglasses, masks and heavy shadow — these are rules about what the
      model returns, not about the adapter, and they belong to the rule engine
      (#21) where they can be worded for the user. The adapter's job is to
      decline when measurement is unreliable, which it does.
  [ ] Warm on pointerenter over the dropzone — needs the dropzone (#26).
  [ ] Integration with the real model in a browser — needs a page that runs an
      analysis (#26).

TESTS
  124 tests across the analysis module, 100% coverage with no suppressions.
  The MediaPipe modules are injected, so the adapter is fully tested without a
  WebAssembly runtime; only the dynamic import itself is excluded, alone in a
  file that does nothing else.

  Worth recording: the column-major matrix read was wrong the first time. In a
  column-major 4x4, element (0,1) is at index 4 and (2,0) at index 2, and the
  row-major intuition puts them at 1 and 8. Both are plausible, and both still
  look like a rotation — the error surfaces only as a head reported as tilted
  when it is turned. The fix names the axes it reads, and the test builds its
  matrices row by row and transposes once.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #18 — Person segmentation and crown detection
Branch: feat/segmentation-crown        Depends on: #17               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  THE RISKIEST PR IN THE PROJECT. Landmarks stop at the hairline, so crown-to-
  chin — the most common rejection reason — depends entirely on this working.
  Built the hard-case corpus first, as this entry required.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] MediaPipe ImageSegmenter behind the detector interface. The 250 KB binary
      person/background model, NOT the 16 MB multiclass one that labels hair
      separately: multiclass would double the whole download to answer a
      question it cannot answer, since the skull is underneath the hair and
      even a perfect hair mask does not say where it is.
  [x] Mask cleanup: open, close, connected components, hole fill — each a pure
      function with its own tests
  [x] Crown estimation from the cleaned mask
  [x] Per-result confidence, plus a mayIncludeCovering flag the rule engine
      must surface rather than swallow

  CROWN DEFINITION IS PER-COUNTRY, and this entry had it wrong
  This plan said "the spec means the skull, not the hair halo". That is true of
  the UK, which says the crown is the top of the head "not including hair", and
  not of US guidance, which measures the head as photographed. The difference
  is the height of someone's hair — tens of millimetres on a voluminous head,
  far outside any tolerance. So it is a property of the specification, and
  estimateCrown takes it as a parameter.
  [ ] OWED: PhotoSpec needs a crownDefinition field, and each verified spec
      needs the right value. Deferred to the spec-verification work (#29)
      rather than guessed at here, since every value in that registry is a
      claim about a government requirement.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Speckle scattered above the head — a single stray pixel is the crown for
      any algorithm that takes the topmost subject pixel in the frame
  [x] Holes punched in the subject, including one reaching the crown row
  [x] Earrings and glasses arms — removed by opening before anything is measured
  [x] Someone else's head, higher in the frame. The most dangerous defect:
      selection is by the component containing the face, never by size or
      position, so a bystander is discarded entirely
  [x] Hats, turbans and hijabs — flagged, and refused outright under the skull
      definition, because no silhouette contains what is underneath them
  [x] Voluminous hair NOT flagged. The discriminator is taper, not width: a
      thick head of hair is as wide as a hat, and width alone would warn every
      person with curly hair about a hat they are not wearing. A skull is a
      dome and narrows at the top; a covering is flat and does not.
  [x] Bald and shaved heads, where the crown is skin
  [x] Crown outside the frame — declines rather than guessing
  [x] A model that eroded the outline
  [x] A subject against the bottom edge, where the taper sample row is off-image
  [x] Malformed masks whose data is shorter than their dimensions
  [x] Segmentation unavailable entirely — landmarks still work and crown height
      degrades to unmeasurable rather than to wrong

  NOT REACHABLE FROM A MASK
  [ ] Dark hair on a dark background, hair blending into clothing, and
      low-contrast sources are properties of the IMAGE, not of the mask. They
      determine whether the model produces a good mask; they cannot be tested
      by feeding masks to the cleanup code. The synthetic image corpus from #6
      already contains them, and they belong to the real-model integration
      test that #26 will make possible.

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  A constructed mask corpus, not the ~30 real photos this entry asked for, and
  the substitution is deliberate. The usable face datasets — FFHQ, CelebA,
  UTKFace, FairFace — are all non-commercially licensed, and beyond the licence
  committing strangers' faces to a repository whose product promises the photo
  never leaves the device is indefensible. More importantly they would be the
  weaker test: hand-measuring a crown from a photograph gives ground truth
  accurate to a few pixels and arguable at the edges, while a constructed mask
  has a crown row that is exactly known. A failure is always the code.

  What a constructed mask cannot show is whether the model produces masks like
  these. That is owed to a real browser and recorded as owed, not pretended.

  To extend the corpus: add a defect function to mask-defect.builder.ts
  alongside addSpeckle, addHole, addBlob and erodeEdge, then assert against
  BASE_MASK_SPEC.crownY. Each function returns a copy, so defects compose.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #19 — Geometry engine
Branch: feat/geometry-engine           Depends on: #18               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Landmarks plus crown plus spec, in. Millimetre measurements and the crop
  rectangle, out. Pure functions — no imaging, fully unit-testable.

  TWO FUNCTIONS, NOT ONE, and the second was not in this entry.
  planCrop answers "make me a compliant photo": it chooses the crop, so the
  head lands mid-band and the face is centred by construction.
  measureFraming answers "is this photo compliant?": the framing is fixed, and
  whether the head is the right size and centred is exactly the question.
  Writing only the first made horizontal centring — which this entry asks for —
  structurally impossible to fail, and a check that cannot fail is not a check.
  Someone checking a photo a booth already produced is a real user of a product
  called a checker.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Head height (crown→chin), eye-line height, horizontal centring
  [x] Roll from the inter-ocular line, shared by both functions
  [x] Ideal crop rectangle satisfying the spec, plus achievability check
  [x] Aspect check on an already-cropped photo: a 51x51 print submitted to a
      Schengen application is the wrong shape before anything about the face
      is considered

  The crop is DERIVED from the head, not chosen and then checked. Printing a
  crop of height H at the spec's physical height fixes the scale, so a target
  head height in millimetres names exactly one crop height. Aiming at the
  middle of the band rather than an edge is deliberate: the edges are where a
  rejection lives, and landmark jitter of a pixel or two between runs would
  otherwise flip a photo from pass to fail with nothing about it changing.

  [ ] NOT DONE — yaw and pitch from the transform matrix. Yaw is already
      recovered in mediapipe-detector, where the matrix is; re-deriving it here
      would mean two sources for one number. Pitch is not extracted anywhere
      yet and belongs with the rule that needs it (#21). Reading the matrix in
      two places is how the two answers drift apart.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Crown above the top edge, chin below the bottom edge, chin above crown
  [x] Required crop extends outside the source, on any side
  [x] Source resolution too low for the spec's DPI — declines rather than
      upscaling, since enlargement invents detail the printer renders as
      softness, itself a rejection reason
  [x] Extreme aspect ratios: a wide panorama crops fine when the head is small
      enough, and is declined when it is not, because height is the constraint
  [x] Head correctly sized but positioned so no valid crop exists
  [x] A head too few pixels tall to measure — the arithmetic still produces a
      crop and every millimetre in it is noise
  [x] Rounding consistent with #14: every value returned is raw, and rounding
      happens only at display, so nothing is nudged across a band edge by
      being formatted on the way through

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  43 unit tests, 100% coverage. Includes the property that matters: across
  head sizes from 200 to 2400 pixels, the measured head height is the band
  midpoint to six decimal places. If that ever fails, the derivation is wrong
  rather than a threshold being off.

  [ ] The PR #18 mask fixtures are not wired in here. They produce a crownY,
      which this engine takes as an input — the seam between them is a single
      number, and testing it twice would test the fixture rather than the
      geometry. They meet in the pipeline assembly (#21).

NOTE
  DEGREES_PER_RADIAN had been written out three times — the pose reader, the
  crop planner and the framing measurer — before this PR gave it a home in
  measurement/angle.constants.ts. Roll had been implemented twice.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #20 — Image quality analysis
Branch: feat/quality-analysis          Depends on: #19               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Background, shadow, sharpness, exposure and resolution checks over the
  segmentation mask.

  THE FAIRNESS REQUIREMENT SHAPED THIS WHOLE MODULE, and it forced one item
  in the list below to be written differently from how it was specified.

  "Exposure: histogram clipping percentage AND MEAN FACE LUMINANCE" cannot be
  built as written. Skin tone IS luminance — a correctly exposed photograph of
  dark skin has a lower mean than a correctly exposed photograph of pale skin,
  by definition and by a wide margin. Any threshold on the mean rejects
  darker-skinned people for having been photographed accurately, and delivers
  it as a confident "your photo is too dark". That is the exact failure that
  made camera metering notorious, and the two requirements in that line
  contradict each other.

  Exposure is therefore judged on two tone-independent signals and the mean is
  never consulted: CLIPPING (pixels pinned at pure black or white have lost
  their detail, whatever tone the subject is) and TONAL RANGE (a well-exposed
  face of any tone has modelling across it; an under- or over-exposed one
  collapses toward one end). The mean is still computed, because the
  background checks need it, and reading it in exposure.utils.ts would
  reintroduce precisely what that file exists to avoid.

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Background uniformity: luminance standard deviation over background pixels
  [x] Background colour against the spec's accepted range
  [x] Shadow detection via a left-to-right luminance difference, not an overall
      spread — a shadow darkens one side, texture darkens everywhere, and
      conflating them sends someone to move a lamp when they need another wall
  [x] Sharpness via Laplacian variance over the face region
  [x] Exposure via clipping and tonal range — see above for why not the mean
  [x] Subject/background separation, as a confidence signal rather than a rule
  [ ] Resolution sufficiency — already built in #14 (evaluateResolution) and
      wired in #19 (planCrop declines rather than upscaling). Rebuilding it
      here would be a second answer to one question.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Patterned and textured backgrounds — measured: amplitude 30 gives a
      standard deviation of 13 against a plain wall's 1.2
  [x] Gradient backgrounds, reported as shadowed rather than uneven
  [x] White clothing on a white wall, and hair the tone of the wall — both
      surface as a low subject/background separation, which is the honest
      answer: the mask bleeds and every measurement through it is suspect
  [x] Motion blur vs shallow depth of field — deliberately NOT distinguished.
      Both reduce the Laplacian identically, the verdict is "soft" and never a
      diagnosis, because the pixels do not say which and a confident wrong
      reason sends someone to fix the wrong thing
  [x] Bright but valid vs genuinely clipped — a high-key photograph that keeps
      its detail passes; one whose highlights have gone does not
  [x] Upscaled low-resolution images that meet pixel counts but are soft —
      caught by the Laplacian, which is what upscaling destroys
  [x] Very small background area when the crop is tight — declines rather than
      reporting a standard deviation from a sliver of corner pixels
  [x] Skin tone never influences the exposure verdict. Six tones from 55 to
      205, asserted identical at the same exposure, with the tonal range
      itself varying by under 6 across them so the agreement is structural and
      not a coincidence of today's thresholds. Also asserted in the negative:
      under-exposure, clipped shadows and clipped highlights each fire at
      every tone, because a check that never fires for dark skin is as broken
      as one that always does.
  [x] Skin tone does not influence sharpness either. Laplacian variance scales
      with local contrast and a dark face has less of it, so this needed
      asserting rather than assuming.
  [ ] Vignetting from a phone camera is not detected. It is a radial falloff,
      and every signal here is either side-to-side or global; adding a radial
      term without a real photograph to calibrate against would be a threshold
      invented from nothing. Owed to the real-model work.

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  41 unit tests, 100% coverage. The fixture is new: the existing head
  generator paints a flat disc of one luminance, which has no modelling and
  therefore cannot express exposure at all — every such face reads as flat.
  toned-face.builder.ts separates baseTone (who is in the photograph) from
  modellingRange (how well it was lit), which is exactly the separation a
  mean-luminance check fails to make.

  Every threshold in this module was measured against that corpus, not
  guessed. The sharpness threshold was moved from 40 to 100 once the numbers
  were in: sharp measures 676 and a one-pixel blur measures 36, so 40 sat four
  away from a boundary a different noise seed could have crossed.

───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PR #21 — Rule engine and compliance report model
Branch: feat/rule-engine               Depends on: #20               Status: DONE
───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DESCRIPTION
  Measurements plus spec, in. A typed, ordered, human-readable verdict out —
  where every failure carries a physical action the user can take.

  DECIDED: rule identities follow the ICAO/ISO portrait-quality requirement
  set, 30 requirements, so our vocabulary matches the authorities' own
  documentation and we are comparable to the published benchmarks instead of
  grading ourselves against a list we invented. 17 of the 30 are measured,
  which is the fidelity matrix's 17 rules landing exactly where planned.

  DEVIATION, and it is deliberate: ISO/IEC 19794-5 is a paid standard and
  nobody here has read it. The identifiers are requirement NAMES transcribed
  from the public literature built on the standard, and nothing in the
  codebase prints a clause number — a fabricated "§7.2.3" beside a verdict
  would be the most convincing thing on the page and the least true. Buying
  the standard and attaching real clause references is owed alongside spec
  verification (#29).

IMPLEMENTS
  [x] Rule definitions with a requirement id, severity, evidence source and
      evaluator — 24 rules, 18 measured and 6 always the reader's
  [x] Coverage map over all 31 requirements (30 ISO + photo age), DERIVED from
      the registry rather than authored beside it, so the published map cannot
      drift from what the engine does. 17 checked, 8 manual, 4 undetectable,
      2 planned — published because a list of only what we check invites the
      reader to assume the rest passed
  [x] Verdicts: pass / fail / warning / manual / undetectable
  [x] Fix instructions computed from the delta, in ratios and degrees
  [x] Overall verdict aggregation, with a precedence ORDER OF ITS OWN, distinct
      from the display order — undetectable ranks second for the headline and
      fourth for the rows, because a report that could not measure something
      is not entitled to summarise itself as a warning
  [x] Manual checklist for glasses, head coverings, veils, hair, print marks
      and recency, worded from the spec's own policy
  [x] Message resolution: labels, findings, measurements, requirements and fix
      instructions, all from the content module, all through Intl

  [~] "Move ~30cm closer" is NOT emitted, and this is a refusal rather than an
      omission. Turning "your head needs to be 20% taller" into a distance
      requires knowing how far away the camera already is; the scale factor
      gives the RATIO of two distances and nothing more. An invented
      centimetre figure would be wrong for anyone not standing where we
      guessed, and it would be the most actionable-looking sentence on the
      page. Instructions are quantified in percentages, degrees and
      millimetres — all of which are measured.

DEFERRALS THAT CAME DUE HERE
  [x] Sunglasses, masks and heavy shadow (deferred from #17 "to be worded for
      the user"). Honoured as coverage rather than as detection: tinted
      lenses, lens glare, heavy frames and frames across the eyes are one
      manual glasses check; a veil over the face is its own; shadow across the
      face is published as undetectable, because telling a cast shadow from
      the modelling that makes a face look like a face is not something
      luminance settles.
  [x] Pitch extraction (deferred from #19 to "the rule that needs it").
      Recovered from the transformation matrix, and the yaw formula corrected
      while there: dividing by R[2][2] alone is the cosine of the yaw only
      while the head is level, so a photo with the chin down had its yaw
      overstated — a straight-on face reported as turned away.
      The pitch RULE reports magnitude only. The sign of this decomposition
      has never been held against a real capture, and a confidently reversed
      "lower your chin" said to somebody who needs to raise it is worse than
      no direction at all.
  [x] mayIncludeCovering surfaced (its own comment said "the rule engine must
      report this rather than swallow it"). Reported by the one advisory rule
      in the registry — a flat-topped silhouette is a hat or it is a head of
      hair, and failing a photograph on that would reject people for their
      hair.

EDGE CASES
  [x] Detection failed entirely does not report "pass" by omission. Structural
      rather than remembered: every rule's missing-input path goes through one
      unmeasured() constructor, and no evaluator has a branch that turns an
      absent measurement into anything else. Asserted by handing the engine an
      empty bundle and requiring that no rule passes.
  [x] Low confidence degrades to manual — in BOTH directions. The obvious half
      is that an unreliable pass is not a pass. The half worth writing down is
      that an unreliable FAILURE is as damaging: it sends somebody to retake a
      photograph that was fine, on a measurement we already decided not to
      trust. Confidence is a property of the evidence, not of the answer.
  [x] Contradictory rules resolved deterministically, by fix group. Only
      framing has one, and it is the case the mechanism exists for: moving
      closer changes the head height AND the eye line AND the centring. The
      earlier rule keeps its instruction; the others keep their verdict and
      their measurement and name the rule that covers them.
  [x] Rules merely fixed at the same time are NOT grouped. "Retake sharper"
      and "retake with more light" compose; suppressing one would lose a real
      finding. Same for the three pose rules — three axes, three independent
      movements.
  [x] A requirement the country never published is omitted, not passed. A row
      reading "Eye position — meets the requirement" against a country that
      publishes no eye position is a verdict on a rule nobody wrote.
  [x] Never emits "guaranteed to pass". Enforced over the whole content tree
      by the existing copy invariants, which the new rule copy now flows
      through — 140-odd strings, each asserted.
  [x] All messages come from the content module, keyed by unions, so a rule
      added without copy fails the build rather than rendering a blank row.

  [ ] The background verdict was ordered and short-circuiting, so a blue
      patterned wall came back as 'wrong-colour' and said nothing about the
      pattern — and a rule reading only the verdict would have reported the
      pattern as acceptable, a pass it never established. Fixed here rather
      than worked around: evaluateBackground now answers its four questions
      independently and the ordered verdict is derived from them.
  [ ] MIN_FACE_LUMINANCE / MAX_FACE_LUMINANCE deleted from analysis.constants,
      with a test asserting no such threshold returns. They were never wired
      to anything, and a band on mean face luminance is a band on skin tone.
      Leaving them in the constants file was an invitation to wire them.

SEO  n/a

TESTS
  132 unit tests across the module, 100% coverage, no suppressions. The
  centrepiece is a scenario table: one entry per state any rule can reach, and
  a final test asserting that every message the content module holds is
  produced by one of them. A message with no path to it is either dead copy or
  an unreachable branch, and neither is visible in a diff.

  No Storybook stories: this PR ships no component. RuleResultRow already has
  its own, and wiring the resolver into a results page belongs to #26 rather
  than being invented here ahead of its design.

